es a 16x8 row of a sprite where the right tile is mirror-flipped. So the layout \"row\" for the Goomba = pairs (FC,FC), (70,71), (72,73)? But if the right tile is always flipped, then the Goomba's row (70,71) means tile 70 on left and tile 71 flipped on right. Hmm, but the Goomba's OAM showed $70 at upper-left and $71 at upper-right with NO flip. Hmm.\n\nActually, the Goomba's OAM: $70 (+0,+8) no flip, $71 (+8,+8) no flip. So the right tile 71 isn't flipped. But this routine ($F282) flips one. Maybe this routine is for a specific sprite class (e.g., the \"mirrored\" sprites). Hmm.\n\nHmm, this is a different table! Because if $EBAA reads pairs from $E73E and draws 16x8 mirrored rows, then the 6-byte Goomba row (FC FC 70 71 72 73) would be 3 pairs: (FC,FC), (70,71), (72,73) = three 16x8 rows: blank, body, feet — with each right tile flipped. But Goomba's actual OAM has no flips and uses tiles 70,71,72,73 directly. Hmm, but if the routine draws 70 (left) and 71 flipped (right), and tile 71 is a mirror of 70, then the result looks symmetric. Is tile 71 the mirror of 70? Possibly! Goomba is symmetric, so the game might store only the left tile and mirror it. Actually the Goomba's actual OAM had no flip, using both 70 and 71, so 70 and 71 are distinct but symmetric halves... hmm, if 71 is exactly the mirror of 70 then using flip wouldn't matter.\n\nHmm, but the routine at $EBAA reads $E73E,X and $E73F,X with the SAME X (adjacent addresses) → that's a 2-byte pair at $E73E+X where X steps by 2. So table entries are 2-byte pairs. Then the \"6-byte rows\" = 3 consecutive pairs.\n\nSo the table structure: pairs of tile ids, 3 pairs per 16x24 sprite. The task's description \"6-byte rows; each row is the tile ids of one enemy frame in drawing order (top left, top right, middle left, middle right, bottom left, bottom right)\" matches: pairs = (top-left, top-right), (middle-left, middle-right), (bottom-left, bottom-right).\n\nAnd the routine at $EBAA/X steps by 2 per pair, and $F282 draws a pair as two tiles with the right one flipped (for sprites whose right tile = mirror of left). Hmm, but Goomba's right tiles aren't flips. So maybe $EBAA is for a different set of sprites with X = 2*something, and the Goomba uses a different routine.\n\nAnyway, this tells me the 6-byte row structure is right.\n\nBut then why do rows 25+ look partial? Because the right tile is flipped when drawn, and also because some sprites' pairs are (blank, tile). Let me re-view rows 29,30 with the knowledge that the right column tiles are flipped. If I mentally mirror the right column, they'd connect better. Indeed, the tool warned \"If it looks mirrored or jumbled, the game may flip these tiles when it draws them.\" So row 29 image: left column was a white/tile shape and right column another shape; when right is flipped they form a coherent sprite! Yes! That explains the \"partial/chaotic\" look.\n\nSo for naming, I should mentally mirror (or use list_sprites-style flips). But the shapes should still be recognizable as half of a symmetric creature.\n\nHmm OK. Let me reconsider rows with that in mind:\n- Row 29 (7D 7C / D1 8C / D3 D2): forms a creature where right column is mirrored. The left column: 7D, D1, D3; right column: 7C, 8C, D2 (flipped). Hmm.\n- Row 31 (D5 D4 / E3 E2 / D3 D2).\n\nThese pairs like (D5,D4) are probably left/right halves of a symmetric sprite.\n\nHmm. Which enemy uses tiles $D0-$D7 and $E2-$E3, $E5-$E6, $EC-$EE? That could be the \"Piranha Plant\"! Piranha Plant: head with white spots, and it's symmetric, so tiles stored as (left,right). Hmm. Or \"Bowser\".\n\nLet me now think about the known SMB Piranha Plant tiles: I recall Piranha Plant uses tiles $D0-$DF? Hmm. Actually I have a vague memory: Piranha Plant graphics are at $D0-$DB? and its two frames.\n\nAnd Bowser uses $C0-$FF? Hmm.\n\nLet me try to identify via spawned enemies. That's the surest way. Let me do the following experiment:\n1. Get to 1-1 with the first Goomba on screen (or a pa